WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.370
- So last week, the presiding judge

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:02.700
in the Derek Chauvin trial,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.490
chastised one of the witnesses,

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:05.810
one of the first responders.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.780
- I will determine when
your answer is done.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.990
- Okay, well...
- Do not argue with the court,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.460
do not argue with counsel.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.350
Answer the questions.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.950
Do not volunteer information
that is not requested.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.060
- Many people reached out and said

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.700
that they thought that the
judge was chastising a witness

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.260
and was interfering in the process.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.390
Actually, this is much more common

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.600
and not very controversial.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.300
A witness has to answer the questions

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.590
that are presented to them.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.540
The opposing attorneys have an opportunity

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.520
to get the other
information that they want

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.820
out of the witness later on.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.680
And that's why in cross examination,

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.630
you're allowed to ask leading questions.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:35.740
But when you're on the witness stand,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.930
the witness should just
answer the questions posed

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.510
and not try to interject
additional information.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:41.700
And that's what the judge thought

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:42.850
that this witness was doing.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.010
So this was not something
that was out of hand.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.210
This is not unusual.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:47.780
Judges do this all the time.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.000
They really just want the trial to proceed

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.200
in an orderly fashion,

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.190
and for all the attorneys

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:52.990
to be able to ask all the
questions that they need to,

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.450
to get the facts out that they need.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.810
So stay tuned for more
coverage of the Chauvin trial.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.353
And I'll see you in court.

